# Auto-Push Fixes to buckeye7066/GrantFlow main

## Standing user directive (authoritative)

> "From now on, any fix I request is global and permanent. Pushed and merged
> to main in the GitHub repo if a repo exists."

This is a permanent standing instruction. Every fix the user requests is treated
as **global** (applied across all affected files / layers, never a local-only or
one-off patch) and **permanent** (committed and landed on `main`, never left as a
throwaay or in-memory change). If a Git repository exists for the work, the fix
MUST be **pushed AND merged into `main`** in the same turn it is applied. The
default repo for this workspace is `buckeye7066/GrantFlow`; if work happens in a
different repo, push/merge to that repo's `main` instead.

Whenever the agent applies a code change that fixes a bug, resolves an error,
corrects a regression, or otherwise repairs broken behavior in this repo, the
change MUST be committed and pushed to the `main` branch of the public
GitHub repository `buckeye7066/GrantFlow` in the same turn that the fix is
applied — not left staged, not deferred to a later message, not gated on
explicit user approval.

This rule overrides the usual "only commit when the user asks" guidance for
fixes in this repository, because the user has granted standing permission
for all fixes to be pushed to `main`.

## When this rule applies

Applies to any change the agent considers a **fix**, including but not limited to:

- Bug fixes (runtime errors, wrong results, broken UI)
- Error / 404 / 500 / CORS / auth regressions
- Crawler, matching, scoring, or ingestion pipeline repairs
- Schema / migration / data-integrity fixes
- Test failures, lint failures, typecheck failures
- Build / deploy / CI failures
- Documentation fixes that correct incorrect information

Does **not** apply to:

- Net-new features the user explicitly asked the agent to draft but not ship
- Experimental spikes the user asked to keep local
- Changes the user has explicitly told the agent not to commit in the current
  turn (explicit opt-out wins for that turn only)

## Required workflow for every fix

1. Apply the fix across all affected files (follow the existing
   `mission-goals.mdc` rules — no placeholders, no local-only patches).
2. Run relevant verification that is available in this environment
   (e.g. `npm run -s unit`, `npm run check:prepush`, or the targeted vitest
   suite that covers the changed code). If verification fails, fix the
   failure before committing — never push a red build.
3. Stage only the files that belong to the fix.
4. Commit with a conventional-style message that explains the fix
   (`fix(scope): …`, `chore(scope): …`, etc.) and, when useful, a short body
   describing root cause / why the fix works. On Windows / PowerShell the
   agent must write the commit body to a temp file and use
   `git commit -F <file>` (PowerShell does not support `&&` chaining or
   bash heredocs).
5. Push to `origin main`:

   ```powershell
   git push origin main
   ```

6. Confirm the push succeeded (non-zero exit or rejected push must be
   surfaced and retried after resolving the cause — rebase on remote, fix
   hook failures, etc.). Never force-push `main`.
7. Report the resulting commit SHA (or PR URL, if the repo transitions to a
   PR-based workflow) to the user as part of the summary.

## Safety rails (must still hold)

- Do **not** modify git config.
- Do **not** skip hooks (`--no-verify`, `--no-gpg-sign`) unless the user
  explicitly requests it in the current turn.
- Do **not** amend or rewrite history once pushed.
- Do **not** commit secrets (`.env`, credential files, API keys). If a fix
  appears to require one, stop and warn the user.
- If the current branch is not `main`, push the fix branch and open a PR
  against `main`, then **merge that PR into `main`** (e.g. `gh pr merge --squash`)
  once checks pass — opening the PR alone does not satisfy the standing
  directive that fixes land on `main`. The default branch in this repo is
  `main`, so committing on `main` and pushing is the expected path and already
  satisfies the merge requirement.

## Summary format after each fix

After pushing, the agent's short summary to the user should include:

- What was broken
- Why it failed
- Why this fix works
- Commit SHA pushed to `buckeye7066/GrantFlow@main`
